(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-rates-disease-measurement-quiz"]={"title":"Rates and Disease Measurement Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why does an age-adjusted death rate differ from a crude one?","choices":["Adjustment removes deaths from unrelated causes","Adjustment converts the rate into a proportion","Adjustment removes the effect of the population's age structure","Adjustment changes the multiplier used"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the difference can be substantial: a crude United States rate of about 794 per 100,000 in 2009 corresponded to an age-adjusted figure of about 741. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"When is a probability sample useful?","choices":["When subgroups such as age or ethnicity must be investigated","When the population is too small to sample at all","When participants must be recruited quickly and cheaply","When every individual must have an equal chance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because a random sample of the whole population may not deliver enough of a small subgroup to say anything about it. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which study designs allow surveillance to support hypothesis testing for etiology?","choices":["Case-control or cohort studies","Cross-sectional surveys only","Randomized controlled trials only","Case reports and case series"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and this is where surveillance hands over to analytic epidemiology: the data identify a question that a comparative design is then built to answer. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why are rates used rather than raw counts?","choices":["Rates are easier to collect than counts","Rates allow comparisons","Rates remove the effect of chance","Rates establish the cause of a disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two places with different populations cannot be compared on counts alone, which is why a denominator and a time period are built into the measure. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does distribution by person involve?","choices":["Mapping where cases occur geographically","Profiling those affected, including behavior as well as demographics","Plotting the number of cases against time","Comparing rates between nations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the questions are deliberately practical: where they eat, where they shop, what products they use, their drinking water, and whether they attended the same event. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Cardiovascular disease accounted for about 24.5 percent of United States deaths in a year. Which measure is that?","choices":["Cause-specific mortality rate","Case fatality","Proportionate mortality","Crude death rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is a fraction rather than a rate, since its denominator is total deaths rather than the population at risk. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Who should be included in the population at risk?","choices":["Only those susceptible to the disease or outcome","Everyone living in the geographic area","Only those who already have the condition","Everyone who consented to take part"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the example is unambiguous: men are not included in the denominator of a study of ovarian cancer. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What did the SchoolNuts study set out to determine?","choices":["The prevalence of food allergy in children aged 10 to 14","Whether a new treatment for food allergy worked","Whether food allergy causes school absence","The incidence of new food allergy over ten years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, using a parent questionnaire together with allergy testing, and finding roughly one in twenty children in Melbourne affected. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are prevalence studies good and bad at?","choices":["Good for establishing cause, poor for planning services","Good for following disease over many years","Good for measuring the severity of a condition","Good for hypotheses and service planning, poor for cause and effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and they are relatively inexpensive, which is part of why they are a sensible first look at what is common in a community. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does provider reporting of morbidity allow?","choices":["Calculation of the case fatality rate","Tracking of possible contacts and exposures","Assignment of patients to control groups","Determination of the pretest probability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is what converts a report of one case into the beginning of an investigation rather than a single data point. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the Framingham Heart Study an example of?","choices":["A prevalence, or cross-sectional, study","A randomized controlled trial","A case-control study","An incidence, or cohort, study"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It began in 1948 with an original cohort, follows multiple generations and additional cohorts, and helped identify the cardiovascular risk factors. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What follows the testing of hypotheses in an outbreak investigation?","choices":["Establishing the case definition","Determining whether an epidemic exists","Initiating control measures","Plotting the epidemic curve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the sequence runs on from there to solving the original source, controlling the outbreak and deciding that it is over. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is a rate?","choices":["The total number of people with a condition","The proportion of deaths attributable to one cause","The number of times an event occurs in a fixed time period","The severity of a disease in those who have it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the reason rates exist rather than raw counts is comparison: a count means nothing until it is set against a population and a period. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why is mortality listed as a surveillance strategy?","choices":["Mortality rates are the most accurate measure of disease","Mortality determines the case definition","Deaths are easier to count than cases","Some cases are detected only at death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which makes death certification a genuine source of case finding rather than merely a record of outcome. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which example is given for documenting geographic distribution and spread?","choices":["Vibrio infections after hurricane floods","Emerging antibiotic resistance in an organism","A person with tuberculosis on an international flight","Influenza in a nursing home resident"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the exposure travels with the person, which makes the geographic question the operative one. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the denominator of the maternal mortality rate?","choices":["The mid-interval population of women","Total deaths from all causes","The number of pregnancies in the interval","Live births in the same interval"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, multiplied by 100,000, with deaths assigned to pregnancy-related causes in the numerator. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which rates are named as specific to maternal and child health?","choices":["Crude and age-specific death rates","Incidence and prevalence rates","Live birth rates and fetal, infant and maternal death rates","Case fatality and death-to-case ratio"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with neonatal and perinatal death rates alongside them, all of which take live births rather than population as their denominator. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which rates are grouped under morbidity rates?","choices":["Crude and cause-specific death rates","Incidence and prevalence rates","Live birth and fetal death rates","Case fatality and years of potential life lost"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Morbidity concerns illness in the living, whereas the mortality group covers crude, age-specific, cause-specific, adjusted and case fatality rates. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is another name for a prevalence study?","choices":["A cohort study","A case-control study","A cross-sectional study","A randomized trial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the population is assessed for the presence of a condition at one point rather than followed for its appearance. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why is the age-adjusted death rate the right measure for comparing a county with its state?","choices":["It counts only deaths among residents","It uses live births as the denominator","It expresses each cause as a percentage of deaths","It removes differences in age structure between the two"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and without it a county with an older population would appear less healthy on the crude rate for that reason alone. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why must an investigation confirm that an epidemic truly exists?","choices":["Epidemics are always reported by clinicians first","An apparent rise may reflect detection rather than occurrence","Endemic disease cannot produce clusters of cases","Attack rates cannot be determined otherwise"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why a baseline and a settled case definition come first: both awareness and definition change how many cases are found. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What must be determined at the start of an outbreak investigation?","choices":["Whether the situation is epidemic or endemic","Which control measures to implement","Which journal will publish the findings","The cost of the response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside determining the attack rate, establishing the diagnosis and the case definition, confirming that an epidemic truly exists, and characterizing it by time, place and person. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why is proportionate mortality not a rate?","choices":["It is measured over too short a period","Its denominator is deaths rather than population","It applies only to a single age group","It cannot be compared between populations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and because every cause is expressed as a percentage of all deaths, the causes must sum to one hundred percent. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why does surveillance need to establish a baseline?","choices":["Because a baseline proves the cause of the disease","Because surveillance is required to be passive","Because baselines determine the case definition","Without it a rise in cases cannot be recognized"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and establishing that baseline is listed among its core purposes, alongside determining the magnitude of a health problem. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What must the causes in a proportionate mortality analysis sum to?","choices":["One hundred percent","The crude death rate","The number of live births","Whatever the case fatality implies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because each cause is expressed as a share of all deaths in the population over the period, so together they account for every death. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why does the case definition matter so much to measured frequency?","choices":["Most clinical information lies on a continuum, not in two boxes","Case definitions are fixed by national agencies","Frequency depends only on the size of the population","Clinical information is almost always binary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so where the line is drawn between case and non-case has a marked effect on how much disease the measurement finds. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which method is described as useful for collecting prevalence information?","choices":["Long-term follow-up of a cohort","Surveys","Randomized assignment to an intervention","Pooling data from published trials"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a survey counts who does and does not have the condition at one time, which is exactly what prevalence requires. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does incidence describe?","choices":["Everyone who has the condition at one moment","The proportion of cases that prove fatal","The distribution of a condition by person and place","New cases arising in previously healthy people"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, over a specified time period. That restriction to the previously healthy is what separates it from prevalence, which counts everyone who has the condition. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is a grab sample?","choices":["Researchers take whoever they can find","A sample in which everyone has an equal chance","A sample in which everyone has a known chance","A sample drawn separately from each subgroup"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside the convenience sample among the non-random approaches, where who ends up in the study is decided by availability. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What defines a random sample?","choices":["Every individual has a known chance of selection","Every individual has an equal chance of selection","Participants are whoever is convenient to obtain","Researchers take whoever they can find"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is what separates it from a probability sample, where the chance of selection is known but need not be equal. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
